          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "For an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult, guidance recommends short-course therapy with an antibiotic that targets the urine and has minimal impact on gut bacteria.\n\nThe commonly described first-line options include nitrofurantoin, trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole (if local resistance is low), and fosfomycin. The choice of antibiotic should consider local resistance patterns. Fluoroquinolones are generally reserved due to their potential for serious side effects and their importance in treating other infections. Beta-lactam antibiotics are considered alternatives, though they may have slightly lower effectiveness. Urine culture is typically reserved for specific situations and is not routinely recommended for uncomplicated cases (Infectious diseases and urology society guidance).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
